<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  Spec 31 §3/§5: the macOS runtime signing profile (fixed pair, pinned
  here until spec 31 §2's manifest derivation lands). The exec-cache
  shape's pair — the tebako-runtime-openjdk profile, with this factory's
  tebako#1117 verdict recorded (docs/build-chain.md's entitlement-plane
  paragraph, dotnet#5):

  NO allow-dyld-environment-variables — DELIBERATELY ABSENT, never an
    omission. The dyld-stripping plane (tebako-runtime-r#5's class: a
    hardened exe whose re-exec needs DYLD_INSERT_LIBRARIES for core IO
    visibility dies shimless on macOS 14 without the grant) does not
    reach the exec-cache shape. The wrapper only mounts, materializes,
    and execs — nothing in its process consumes the interpose dylib, and
    a stripped self-insert is the product's designed loud fallback
    (proven: the same product binary in the openjdk line runs green on
    darwin-23 hardened with NO entitlements at all). The insertion that
    DOES matter — the preload shim's jail binding across the exec into
    the muxer — is governed by MICROSOFT's signature on the muxer (the
    pinned SDK's dotnet is hardened-signed and carries
    allow-dyld-environment-variables itself), never by this profile.
  disable-library-validation — every runtime slice: a hardened-runtime
    process by default refuses unsigned dylibs, and the driver
    materializes in-image native objects (the preload shim
    libtfs_preload.dylib) to the host for dlopen/injection; they carry
    no Developer ID signature of their own (§1.4). This grant governs
    only WHICH dylibs may load — it says nothing about WHETHER dyld
    honors the environment (the conflation that shipped the
    tebako-runtime-r#5 class).
  allow-jit — a grant, never a requirement: the wrapper never JITs
    (CoreCLR's JIT runs in the exec'd muxer process, under Microsoft's
    own signature and entitlements). The pair rides the exe so a future
    line never needs a profile re-review.

  Keep the two <true/> values on their own lines: the embedding check
  in ci/macos-sign-notarize-runtime.sh counts them (macOS 15 emits the
  embedded plist single-line, so it greps for <true/>, not key names).
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
  <key>com.apple.security.cs.disable-library-validation</key>
  <true/>
  <key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-jit</key>
  <true/>
</dict>
</plist>
